MACs simultaneously authenticated on one physical port**, two of them driven by scapy and one by
`wpa_supplicant`, and the dataplane went 0/4 → 4/4 for the MAC that authenticated last while the
other two stayed up. Note the table shows `ckmulti1` twice, on two different MACs: **one RADIUS
identity may hold several concurrent supplicant sessions**, which the server did not object to.

## 🔴 The finding: `auth host-mode multi-supplicant` SILENTLY REWRITES `dot1x control-direction`

Not something the case asked for, and the more worth reporting for that.

**What was configured.** Case 1 set `dot1x control-direction both` on port1.0.48 and it was in the
running-config, verified. This case never touched `control-direction` at all.

**What came back.** After `auth host-mode multi-supplicant`:

```
awplus#show running-config interface port1.0.48
interface port1.0.48
 switchport
 switchport mode access
 switchport access vlan 48
 dot1x port-control auto
 dot1x control-direction in              <- NOBODY TYPED THIS
 auth host-mode multi-supplicant
```

and `show dot1x interface port1.0.48` agrees: `CD: adminControlledDirections: in`. **No warning,
no `% ` line, no log entry.** A configuration line the operator entered was changed by a later,
different command, and the only way to find out is to re-read the running-config.

**And removing the feature does NOT put it back.** This is the half that makes it matter:

```
awplus(config-if)#no auth host-mode
awplus#show running-config interface port1.0.48
 dot1x port-control auto
 dot1x control-direction in              <- STILL `in`
awplus#show dot1x interface port1.0.48
  CD: adminControlledDirections: in
  hostMode: single-host                  <- host mode DID revert
```

So `hostMode` reverts cleanly but `control-direction` stays downgraded. Anyone who enables
multi-supplicant and later disables it is left with ingress-only control on that port and nothing
anywhere tells them.

**Is it reasonable?** Technically yes — egress on a shared port cannot be gated per-MAC, so
multi-supplicant forcing ingress-only control is defensible. **The silence is the defect**, and the
non-restoration on removal more so: an operator who chose `both` for a security reason has had it
weakened permanently without being told. This is the same shape as the known AW+ behaviour where a
`spanning-tree mode` change silently drops `no spanning-tree <mode> enable`.

**It does not affect any verdict in this case or in case 1.** Case 1's measurements were all taken
with `both` genuinely in force, before this case existed. I restored it explicitly afterwards
(`dot1x control-direction both`, re-read and confirmed) so the next case starts from the state
case 1 left.

