## Verdict

Six legs, every one matching its declared expectation. The feature-OFF baseline admits exactly one
supplicant and leaves the second in silence; turning the feature on admits both, each with its own
complete EAP conversation and its own authorised state on the DUT; a third MAC with a bad password
is refused in the same 1.04 s window while the two good ones succeed; and authorization is proved
to be per-MAC rather than per-port, because the host's own unauthenticated MAC stays blocked at
0/4 until it authenticates in its own right, after which three different MACs are simultaneously
authorised on the one port.

VERDICT: PASS — `auth host-mode multi-supplicant` authenticates multiple supplicants independently on one port (single-host: 1 of 2 admitted, the second silent; multi-supplicant: 2 of 2, then 3 of 3 MACs Authenticated), with a bad-credential control refused in the same packet window and authorization proved per-MAC (unauthenticated host MAC 0/4 → 4/4 once it authenticates). Reported alongside: enabling multi-supplicant silently rewrites `dot1x control-direction both` to `in`, and removing it does not restore `both`.
